From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cristian Papa <pcristian292@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 03:51:38 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] net: xpon: omci: limit the objects the MIB holds

The MIB is an xarray keyed by class and instance, and nothing limited
how many objects it holds. omci_me_active() accepts every class below
240, 256 to 349 and 400 to 452 besides the described ones, and a Create
is allowed on any of them that has no descriptor, so the OLT can create
65536 instances of each of some 380 classes. Every object is a
kmalloc-2k allocation: about 60000 Creates use the 128 MB this router
has free, and the kernel cannot give that memory back. With permissive
or fake OMCI a Get of an unknown instance stores an object too.

Hold at most 4096 objects. A provisioned HGU holds a few hundred (457
here, against a Huawei OLT). A Create on a full MIB is refused with a
processing error before the hardware is touched, and
omci_mib_store_locked() refuses to add a new key, which covers the
objects a Set or a Get creates and the ones written from userspace.
The objects the ONU creates itself (defaults and profile seeds) are
exempt, so a profile change still finds room: their number is fixed by
the code.

The service reconciliation still walks the whole MIB once for every
pair of UNI-side and ANI-side bridge port and priority, so an OLT that
fills the MIB with bridge ports can keep the agent busy for a long
time. The limit bounds that, it does not make it cheap.

Found by reading the source in a security review of the OMCI agent.

Signed-off-by: Cristian Papa <pcristian292@gmail.com>
Co-Authored-By: Claude Mythos 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
--- a/net/xpon/omci/agent.c
+++ b/net/xpon/omci/agent.c
@@ -102,6 +102,14 @@ static bool omci_agent_result_can_be_fak
 #define OMCI_DEFAULT_QUEUES_PER_TCONT		1
 #define OMCI_MAX_TCONT_RESOURCES		32
 #define OMCI_MAX_QUEUES_PER_TCONT		8
+/*
+ * The most objects the MIB holds. Each one takes a kmalloc-2k slot and the
+ * OLT may create any instance of every class the agent accepts, so with no
+ * limit a faulty or hostile OLT fills the memory of the router. A
+ * provisioned HGU holds a few hundred. The objects the ONU creates itself
+ * are always stored: their number is fixed by the code.
+ */
+#define OMCI_MIB_MAX_OBJECTS		4096
 
 #define OMCI_OMCI_ME_TYPE_TABLE_MASK		BIT(15)
 #define OMCI_OMCI_MESSAGE_TYPE_TABLE_MASK	BIT(14)
@@ -672,12 +680,32 @@ static void omci_agent_reset_alarm_seque
 	spin_unlock(&agent->alarm_lock);
 }
 
+static bool omci_mib_full_locked(struct omci_agent *agent)
+{
+	struct omci_mib_object *object;
+	unsigned int count = 0;
+	unsigned long index;
+
+	xa_for_each(&agent->mib, index, object)
+		if (++count >= OMCI_MIB_MAX_OBJECTS)
+			return true;
+
+	return false;
+}
+
 static int omci_mib_store_locked(struct omci_agent *agent,
 				 const struct omci_mib_object *source)
 {
 	struct omci_mib_object *object;
 	void *old;
 
+	/* Replacing a stored object does not make the MIB any larger. */
+	if (source->origin != OMCI_MIB_ORIGIN_DEFAULT &&
+	    !xa_load(&agent->mib,
+		     omci_mib_key(source->class_id, source->entity_id)) &&
+	    omci_mib_full_locked(agent))
+		return -ENOSPC;
+
 	object = kmemdup(source, sizeof(*object), GFP_KERNEL);
 	if (!object)
 		return -ENOMEM;
@@ -3941,6 +3969,13 @@ static u8 omci_agent_create_locked(struc
 		return OMCI_RESULT_NOT_SUPPORTED;
 	if (omci_mib_lookup(agent, class_id, entity_id))
 		return OMCI_RESULT_INSTANCE_EXISTS;
+	/* Refuse before the hardware is touched; it is not a passing state. */
+	if (omci_mib_full_locked(agent)) {
+		dev_warn_ratelimited(odev->parent,
+				     "OMCI MIB full (%u objects): Create of class %u instance %#x refused\n",
+				     OMCI_MIB_MAX_OBJECTS, class_id, entity_id);
+		return OMCI_RESULT_PROCESSING_ERROR;
+	}
 
 	desc = omci_me_lookup(agent, class_id);
 	object = kzalloc(sizeof(*object), GFP_KERNEL);
